# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

## Goal
Generate a PCA of the RNA-seq samples from gene counts, coloured by condition.
Show sample relationships on PC1 and PC2, with sample labels and the percentage of variance explained by each axis.

## Inputs
Resolve both inputs at execution time using `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`; stop if resolution fails. Paths below are relative to the project and reflect current resolution. Inputs remain read-only.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The design currently contains six samples: three untreated and three treated.

## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. All analysis runs in the batch job using the existing `gars-bio` Python environment, sourced through `_system/gars-env.sh`; use pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and matplotlib (Agg backend). No packages will be installed. Stop if a dependency is unavailable.
2. Resolve inputs as above, then load the tab-separated native gene counts and CSV design with pandas. Use `gene_id` as the gene identifier and exclude `gene_name` from numeric data. Require unique, nonempty gene and sample identifiers, finite nonnegative counts, nonempty condition labels, and exact agreement between count sample columns and design sample IDs. Reorder columns to design order. Stop on invalid data, zero library totals, fewer than three samples, or fewer than two usable genes. Preserve fractional counts; do not round them.
3. Retain genes with counts of at least 10 in at least 3 samples. Compute each sample's library total from all input genes, convert retained counts to counts per million (CPM = count / library total * 1,000,000), and transform with log2(CPM + 1). Remove genes with zero variance across samples. Use all remaining genes, without differential-expression selection or condition-dependent feature selection. Record the gene totals before and after each filter.
4. Transpose to samples by genes and run `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=2, svd_solver="full", whiten=False)`. PCA centres each gene; do not scale genes to unit variance. Stop if fewer than two nonzero principal components can be computed. Conditions are used only for plotting and output annotations, not PCA fitting. No batch correction, sample exclusion or statistical testing is planned.
5. Export sample ID, condition, PC1 and PC2 scores, plus a separate table of explained variance and explained variance ratios for both PCs. Plot PC1 against PC2, colour by condition (untreated: #0072B2; treated: #D55E00), label every point with its sample ID, and include a condition legend and variance percentages in axis labels. Save PNG (300 dpi) and PDF versions.
6. Write a brief Markdown methods and QC report containing resolved input paths and checksums, package versions, sample and condition counts, filter counts, library totals, transformation and PCA parameters, and variance percentages. Describe only these diagnostics; clustering is exploratory and does not establish a condition effect.

